1
00:00:00,805 --> 00:00:05,770
Meera runs a bakery in Pune. Her
books say she made ₹12 lakh this year.

2
00:00:05,820 --> 00:00:09,185
So why will she pay tax
on almost nineteen lakh?

3
00:00:09,855 --> 00:00:13,280
Nobody cheated. Her accountant
measures profit one way.

4
00:00:13,595 --> 00:00:16,200
The Income-tax Act
measures it another way.

5
00:00:16,485 --> 00:00:18,970
The gap between the two
is this whole chapter.

6
00:00:19,498 --> 00:00:22,418
The law asks three
questions about every rupee.

7
00:00:22,603 --> 00:00:25,718
Is it business income at
all? Is this cost allowed?

8
00:00:25,973 --> 00:00:27,363
And is it allowed this year?

9
00:00:27,868 --> 00:00:29,753
We'll answer them from first principles.

10
00:00:30,158 --> 00:00:33,613
What gets taxed. What you can
deduct, and what you can't.

11
00:00:33,738 --> 00:00:35,493
The shortcuts for small businesses.

12
00:00:35,963 --> 00:00:39,753
And at the end, Meera's full
computation, line by line.

13
00:00:40,206 --> 00:00:47,186
Everything follows the Income-tax Act,
1961, as amended by the Finance Act, 2025.

14
00:00:48,116 --> 00:00:54,336
That's assessment year 2026-27,
for the January 2027 exam.

15
00:00:55,436 --> 00:00:58,701
Meera bakes cakes. Her
friend Kabir fixes teeth.

16
00:00:58,931 --> 00:01:00,771
Is a dentist running a business?

17
00:01:01,569 --> 00:01:05,734
First, the big picture. The Act
sorts all income into five heads.

18
00:01:06,049 --> 00:01:08,274
Salaries, house property, business or

19
00:01:08,324 --> 00:01:10,954
profession, capital gains,
and other sources.

20
00:01:11,399 --> 00:01:14,754
Each head has its own rules.
Ours is the third one.

21
00:01:15,243 --> 00:01:18,288
Business means any trade,
commerce or manufacture,

22
00:01:18,633 --> 00:01:20,728
or any adventure in the nature of trade.

23
00:01:21,163 --> 00:01:22,638
That's section 2(13).

24
00:01:23,444 --> 00:01:25,689
A profession is work
that needs learning, like

25
00:01:25,739 --> 00:01:28,849
a doctor, a lawyer,
an architect or an auditor.

26
00:01:29,239 --> 00:01:32,799
It includes a vocation
too. That's section 2(36).

27
00:01:33,759 --> 00:01:38,134
Here's the good news. For computing
income, the difference hardly matters.

28
00:01:38,599 --> 00:01:40,834
Business and profession are taxed alike.

29
00:01:41,079 --> 00:01:43,074
It matters only for tax audit and the

30
00:01:43,124 --> 00:01:45,794
presumptive schemes,
which have different limits.

31
00:01:46,294 --> 00:01:50,199
Business usually means doing
something again and again, for profit.

32
00:01:50,634 --> 00:01:52,299
But even one deal can count.

33
00:01:52,804 --> 00:01:56,079
Buy a truckload of mangoes
once, just to resell them,

34
00:01:56,209 --> 00:01:58,609
and that's an adventure
in the nature of trade.

35
00:01:59,202 --> 00:02:01,167
What if the business itself is illegal?

36
00:02:01,527 --> 00:02:06,027
Its profit is still taxed. The law
doesn't ask where income came from.

37
00:02:06,207 --> 00:02:08,917
But money spent on an
offence is never deductible.

38
00:02:09,724 --> 00:02:14,144
Two more rules. Profit received in
kind is valued at its cash worth.

39
00:02:14,499 --> 00:02:17,084
And if you run three
businesses, each one's profit

40
00:02:17,134 --> 00:02:19,974
is worked out separately,
then added together.

41
00:02:20,477 --> 00:02:22,757
Kabir runs a clinic and a medical shop.

42
00:02:22,957 --> 00:02:24,222
Two heads of income?

43
00:02:27,020 --> 00:02:30,285
No. Both fall under one head,
business or profession.

44
00:02:30,830 --> 00:02:33,605
Their profits are worked
out separately, then added.

45
00:02:34,786 --> 00:02:38,086
A company pays Meera
₹5 lakh to promise she

46
00:02:38,136 --> 00:02:40,806
won't open a bakery in
its area for three years.

47
00:02:41,256 --> 00:02:44,426
She baked nothing for it.
Is it business income?

48
00:02:45,132 --> 00:02:48,117
Is a promise not to do
business, business income?

49
00:02:50,478 --> 00:02:52,763
Yes. Section 28 is a net.

50
00:02:53,143 --> 00:02:58,103
It lists everything taxed under this head,
and it reaches well past ordinary profit.

51
00:02:58,611 --> 00:03:00,731
That payment is a non-compete fee.

52
00:03:01,096 --> 00:03:02,631
Money for not carrying on a business,

53
00:03:02,681 --> 00:03:06,116
or for not sharing know-how,
is business income.

54
00:03:06,721 --> 00:03:09,801
The net also catches
compensation, when an agency

55
00:03:09,851 --> 00:03:13,021
or a business contract
is ended or changed.

56
00:03:13,476 --> 00:03:16,621
It catches export incentives,
like duty drawback.

57
00:03:17,046 --> 00:03:21,391
And a trade association's income
from special services to its members.

58
00:03:21,915 --> 00:03:23,920
Benefits in kind count too.

59
00:03:24,330 --> 00:03:28,520
A company gives its lawyer a free
flat, in return for legal work.

60
00:03:28,895 --> 00:03:32,415
The value of that flat is the
lawyer's professional income.

61
00:03:33,005 --> 00:03:35,585
A partner's salary, bonus, commission and

62
00:03:35,635 --> 00:03:38,265
interest from the firm
are business income too.

63
00:03:38,680 --> 00:03:41,265
But only the part the firm
was allowed to deduct.

64
00:03:41,755 --> 00:03:45,700
His share of the firm's profit
is exempt, under section 10(2A).

65
00:03:46,202 --> 00:03:50,027
Three more. Money received
on a keyman insurance policy.

66
00:03:50,482 --> 00:03:53,667
The market value of stock
turned into a capital asset.

67
00:03:53,717 --> 00:03:59,057
And money from an asset whose whole
cost was deducted under section 35AD.

68
00:03:59,595 --> 00:04:01,360
One thing slips out of the net.

69
00:04:01,675 --> 00:04:04,680
Rent from letting out a
residential house is taxed under

70
00:04:04,730 --> 00:04:07,770
house property, even when
letting is your business.

71
00:04:08,520 --> 00:04:13,625
A distributor's agency is cancelled,
and she gets ₹8 lakh as compensation.

72
00:04:14,265 --> 00:04:15,265
Which head?

73
00:04:17,760 --> 00:04:19,305
Business or profession.

74
00:04:20,275 --> 00:04:24,175
Compensation on the end of an
agency is caught by section 28.

75
00:04:25,419 --> 00:04:29,724
Rohan buys shares in the morning,
and sells them before the market closes.

76
00:04:30,214 --> 00:04:34,094
He never takes delivery. Is that
the same business as the bakery?

77
00:04:34,892 --> 00:04:37,657
No. When a deal is settled
without the goods or shares

78
00:04:37,707 --> 00:04:41,557
ever changing hands, it's
a bet on prices, not trade.

79
00:04:42,052 --> 00:04:46,812
The law calls it a speculative
transaction, under section 43(5).

80
00:04:47,132 --> 00:04:49,412
And it's treated as a business of its own.

81
00:04:50,003 --> 00:04:52,328
Why keep it separate? Because of losses.

82
00:04:52,783 --> 00:04:57,468
If you lose on speculation, you can set
it off only against speculation profit.

83
00:04:57,833 --> 00:05:00,493
And you can carry it
forward for just four years.

84
00:05:01,001 --> 00:05:03,881
Some deals without delivery
are not speculation.

85
00:05:04,336 --> 00:05:06,361
A baker hedging the price of wheat.

86
00:05:06,746 --> 00:05:08,561
An investor hedging his shares.

87
00:05:08,931 --> 00:05:11,041
And trading in derivatives, or commodity

88
00:05:11,091 --> 00:05:14,311
derivatives, on a
recognised stock exchange.

89
00:05:14,824 --> 00:05:21,189
Rohan loses ₹50,000 on intraday trades,
while the bakery makes ₹12 lakh.

90
00:05:21,609 --> 00:05:24,149
That loss can't cut the bakery's profit.

91
00:05:24,554 --> 00:05:27,069
It waits for a future speculation profit.

92
00:05:27,699 --> 00:05:31,124
Now picture a tea estate.
Growing the leaves is farming.

93
00:05:31,304 --> 00:05:33,264
Making tea in the factory is business.

94
00:05:33,654 --> 00:05:35,594
One profit, with two natures.

95
00:05:36,249 --> 00:05:38,534
The rules split this composite income.

96
00:05:38,984 --> 00:05:43,054
For tea grown and made by the
seller, 40% is business income.

97
00:05:43,454 --> 00:05:46,889
The other 60% is agricultural, and exempt.

98
00:05:47,495 --> 00:05:52,255
So a tea estate earning ₹10
lakh pays tax on ₹4 lakh.

99
00:05:52,595 --> 00:05:58,875
For rubber, 35% is business.
For coffee grown and cured, 25%.

100
00:05:58,925 --> 00:06:02,525
If it's also roasted and ground, 40%.

101
00:06:03,058 --> 00:06:07,128
Is futures trading on the NSE
a speculative transaction?

102
00:06:09,992 --> 00:06:12,597
No. Eligible trading in derivatives on a

103
00:06:12,647 --> 00:06:15,947
recognised stock exchange
is not speculation.

104
00:06:17,250 --> 00:06:20,115
Meera delivers a wedding cake on 28 March.

105
00:06:20,490 --> 00:06:22,635
The money arrives on 10 April.

106
00:06:22,950 --> 00:06:25,110
Which year does that income belong to?

107
00:06:25,838 --> 00:06:27,253
This year, or next?

108
00:06:29,604 --> 00:06:31,584
It depends on her method of accounting.

109
00:06:32,074 --> 00:06:35,404
Under section 145,
income is computed by the

110
00:06:35,454 --> 00:06:39,034
method she regularly follows,
cash or mercantile.

111
00:06:39,520 --> 00:06:42,240
Under the mercantile system,
income counts when

112
00:06:42,290 --> 00:06:45,000
it's earned, so the cake
belongs to this year.

113
00:06:45,410 --> 00:06:48,420
Under the cash system,
it counts when the money arrives.

114
00:06:48,915 --> 00:06:50,085
That's next year.

115
00:06:50,681 --> 00:06:52,846
Government money follows its own clock.

116
00:06:53,211 --> 00:06:56,306
A subsidy, grant or cash
incentive is taxed in

117
00:06:56,356 --> 00:06:58,901
the year it's received,
whatever her method.

118
00:06:59,637 --> 00:07:00,942
Now the big picture.

119
00:07:01,497 --> 00:07:06,682
Section 29 says business income is
worked out under sections 30 to 43D.

120
00:07:07,127 --> 00:07:10,287
The profit and loss account
is only the starting point.

121
00:07:10,781 --> 00:07:13,306
Here's the machine every
exam question uses.

122
00:07:13,666 --> 00:07:17,226
Start with net profit. Add back
what the law doesn't allow.

123
00:07:17,486 --> 00:07:19,606
Deduct what it allows
but the books missed.

124
00:07:19,936 --> 00:07:23,561
Remove other heads' income.
Then add deemed income.

125
00:07:24,164 --> 00:07:26,164
Think of a security check at the airport.

126
00:07:26,544 --> 00:07:30,584
Every item in the profit and loss
account goes through. Most pass.

127
00:07:30,864 --> 00:07:34,284
Some are taken out. And a few
are added that you never packed.

128
00:07:34,714 --> 00:07:39,424
Unlike security, a disallowed cost is
often just allowed in a later year.

129
00:07:40,026 --> 00:07:43,711
Meera's books include
₹30,000 of bank interest.

130
00:07:44,081 --> 00:07:45,691
What does the machine do with it?

131
00:07:48,481 --> 00:07:53,136
It takes it out. Bank interest is
taxed under income from other sources.

132
00:07:54,338 --> 00:07:59,243
Meera spends ₹4 lakh on flour,
and ₹4 lakh on a new oven.

133
00:07:59,353 --> 00:08:01,183
Both leave her bank this year.

134
00:08:01,233 --> 00:08:02,773
Can she deduct both?

135
00:08:03,432 --> 00:08:04,887
Both, one, or neither?

136
00:08:07,244 --> 00:08:10,464
Only the flour. It's used
up making this year's cakes.

137
00:08:10,774 --> 00:08:12,604
The oven will bake for ten years.

138
00:08:12,899 --> 00:08:14,864
If you charged it all
to one year, this year

139
00:08:14,914 --> 00:08:17,409
would look poor, and the next nine rich.

140
00:08:17,913 --> 00:08:21,153
Spending that's used up within
the year is revenue expenditure.

141
00:08:21,603 --> 00:08:26,053
You can deduct it. Spending with a
lasting benefit is capital expenditure.

142
00:08:26,563 --> 00:08:28,263
You can't deduct it directly.

143
00:08:28,899 --> 00:08:30,684
Repairs sit on this line too.

144
00:08:31,154 --> 00:08:36,184
Section 30 allows rent, rates, taxes,
repairs and insurance of the building.

145
00:08:36,654 --> 00:08:39,184
Section 31 allows current repairs and

146
00:08:39,234 --> 00:08:42,114
insurance of machinery,
plant and furniture.

147
00:08:42,614 --> 00:08:47,859
Repainting the shop, or fixing the oven
door, is a current repair. Deductible.

148
00:08:48,254 --> 00:08:51,539
Replacing the whole oven,
or adding a new floor, is capital.

149
00:08:51,829 --> 00:08:53,184
Not a repair at all.

150
00:08:53,780 --> 00:08:58,720
A few details. An owner using her
own shop can't deduct notional rent.

151
00:08:59,070 --> 00:09:02,700
A firm paying rent to its own
partner can, if it's reasonable.

152
00:09:03,175 --> 00:09:06,530
And a machine used for even a
month gets its full insurance.

153
00:09:07,064 --> 00:09:09,949
What if an asset is used
partly for personal work?

154
00:09:10,169 --> 00:09:12,609
Meera takes the van on family trips.

155
00:09:12,939 --> 00:09:17,244
Under section 38, only the business
share of its costs is allowed.

156
00:09:17,954 --> 00:09:20,399
Meera rebuilds a collapsed
wall of her shop.

157
00:09:20,629 --> 00:09:22,109
Revenue or capital?

158
00:09:24,941 --> 00:09:28,681
Capital. Rebuilding is a
replacement, not a current repair.

159
00:09:29,883 --> 00:09:34,553
If the oven can't be deducted,
does Meera just lose that ₹4 lakh?

160
00:09:35,191 --> 00:09:40,036
No. You get the cost back slowly,
a slice every year, as the oven wears out.

161
00:09:40,371 --> 00:09:43,826
That slice is depreciation,
under section 32.

162
00:09:44,326 --> 00:09:46,086
But only some assets qualify.

163
00:09:46,531 --> 00:09:50,066
Tangible ones: buildings,
machinery, plant and furniture.

164
00:09:50,401 --> 00:09:53,106
And intangible ones: know-how, patents,

165
00:09:53,156 --> 00:09:56,276
copyrights, trademarks,
licences and franchises.

166
00:09:56,903 --> 00:09:58,283
Two famous exceptions.

167
00:09:58,743 --> 00:10:03,303
Land never wears out, so it gets no
depreciation, even under a building.

168
00:10:03,728 --> 00:10:06,533
And goodwill of a business
isn't depreciable any more.

169
00:10:07,400 --> 00:10:08,900
Plant is a wide word.

170
00:10:09,165 --> 00:10:13,600
It includes vehicles, books, scientific
apparatus and surgical equipment.

171
00:10:13,890 --> 00:10:16,740
But not tea bushes,
livestock or buildings.

172
00:10:17,135 --> 00:10:20,190
And roads, bridges and
wells count as buildings.

173
00:10:20,749 --> 00:10:25,014
Then three conditions. She must
own the asset, wholly or partly.

174
00:10:25,389 --> 00:10:27,534
She must use it for
business during the year.

175
00:10:27,694 --> 00:10:29,604
And it must be one of those assets.

176
00:10:30,208 --> 00:10:32,188
Use includes standby use.

177
00:10:32,643 --> 00:10:38,068
A backup generator that never ran, or a
fire extinguisher on the wall, is in use.

178
00:10:38,518 --> 00:10:40,608
And a tenant who builds
a store room in a rented

179
00:10:40,658 --> 00:10:44,308
shop gets depreciation on
it, as if she owned it.

180
00:10:44,906 --> 00:10:48,851
What if Meera simply skips depreciation
this year, to save it for later?

181
00:10:49,231 --> 00:10:53,771
She can't. Under Explanation 5,
depreciation is compulsory.

182
00:10:54,186 --> 00:10:56,566
It's deducted whether
she claims it or not.

183
00:10:57,277 --> 00:10:59,202
Meera leases a mixer from a dealer.

184
00:10:59,252 --> 00:11:01,457
Who claims depreciation on it?

185
00:11:04,191 --> 00:11:08,076
The dealer, who owns it. Meera
deducts the lease rent instead.

186
00:11:09,237 --> 00:11:13,802
Meera's bakery has ovens,
mixers, trays, fridges and a van.

187
00:11:14,277 --> 00:11:16,522
Does she track the
depreciation of each one?

188
00:11:17,231 --> 00:11:20,656
Imagine doing that for a factory
with five thousand machines.

189
00:11:21,016 --> 00:11:23,476
Each with its own date, cost and value.

190
00:11:23,786 --> 00:11:25,396
The paperwork would never end.

191
00:11:25,886 --> 00:11:27,311
So the Act pools them.

192
00:11:27,656 --> 00:11:31,971
A block of assets is a group of assets
in the same class, with the same rate.

193
00:11:32,573 --> 00:11:35,213
Depreciation is then
charged on the whole pool,

194
00:11:35,263 --> 00:11:38,073
at a fixed share of
its written down value.

195
00:11:38,518 --> 00:11:41,783
That's what's left of the cost,
after earlier depreciation.

196
00:11:42,518 --> 00:11:46,403
Here are the main rates.
Residential buildings, 5%.

197
00:11:46,643 --> 00:11:50,983
Other buildings, 10%.
Furniture and fittings, 10%.

198
00:11:51,273 --> 00:11:55,013
Plant and machinery, 15%,
and that includes cars.

199
00:11:55,683 --> 00:12:01,243
And the higher ones. Buses, lorries
and taxis in a hiring business, 30%.

200
00:12:01,293 --> 00:12:06,763
Computers and software, 40%.
Books for professionals, 40%.

201
00:12:06,813 --> 00:12:09,928
Intangible assets, 25%.

202
00:12:10,531 --> 00:12:12,336
A block works like a water tank.

203
00:12:12,536 --> 00:12:15,756
New assets pour in. Sale money drains out.

204
00:12:16,006 --> 00:12:20,016
Every year, a fixed share of what's
inside is taken as depreciation.

205
00:12:20,681 --> 00:12:23,456
Unlike water, the level
can never go below zero.

206
00:12:23,856 --> 00:12:25,266
We'll see what happens then.

207
00:12:25,870 --> 00:12:29,935
A new laptop and a new oven,
both bought this year. Same block?

208
00:12:32,735 --> 00:12:36,940
No. The laptop is at 40%, the oven at 15%.

209
00:12:36,990 --> 00:12:38,990
Different rates, different blocks.

210
00:12:40,229 --> 00:12:42,714
Meera buys a mixer on 20 December.

211
00:12:43,114 --> 00:12:46,834
Should she get a full year's
depreciation, for three months of use?

212
00:12:47,517 --> 00:12:50,422
That seems unfair. So the
Act has a simple rule.

213
00:12:50,722 --> 00:12:53,822
Count the days the asset is
used, in the year it's bought.

214
00:12:54,331 --> 00:12:56,931
If it's used for less
than 180 days in that

215
00:12:56,981 --> 00:12:59,991
year, depreciation is
half the normal rate.

216
00:13:00,406 --> 00:13:03,331
For 180 days or more, it's the full rate.

217
00:13:03,935 --> 00:13:09,080
Let's work it out. Her plant and machinery
block starts the year at ₹10 lakh.

218
00:13:09,480 --> 00:13:13,080
On 1 June, she adds a
new oven for ₹4 lakh.

219
00:13:13,455 --> 00:13:16,775
On 20 December, a mixer for ₹2 lakh.

220
00:13:17,349 --> 00:13:21,149
The oven is used for ten months,
so it joins the full-rate part.

221
00:13:21,514 --> 00:13:24,209
The mixer is used for just
a hundred and two days.

222
00:13:24,564 --> 00:13:27,144
That's under 180, so it gets half.

223
00:13:27,653 --> 00:13:32,513
So 15% of ₹14 lakh is ₹2,10,000.

224
00:13:32,703 --> 00:13:38,013
And 7.5% of ₹2 lakh is ₹15,000.

225
00:13:38,203 --> 00:13:42,503
Total depreciation, ₹2,25,000.

226
00:13:43,527 --> 00:13:49,467
The block carries ₹16 lakh,
minus ₹2,25,000, into next year.

227
00:13:49,802 --> 00:13:55,227
That's ₹13,75,000. And the mixer's
half rate was a one-time thing.

228
00:13:55,622 --> 00:13:57,537
Next year, it's at the full rate.

229
00:13:58,042 --> 00:14:00,422
The rule is about use, not purchase.

230
00:14:00,842 --> 00:14:05,862
A machine bought in April but installed
in November is used for under 180 days.

231
00:14:06,182 --> 00:14:07,182
Half rate.

232
00:14:07,734 --> 00:14:11,639
Write it like this. The asset was
acquired during the previous year.

233
00:14:11,904 --> 00:14:14,939
It was put to use for less than 180 days.

234
00:14:15,279 --> 00:14:19,539
So depreciation is restricted
to 50% of the prescribed rate.

235
00:14:20,298 --> 00:14:24,178
A laptop is bought and put
to use on 1 January. Rate?

236
00:14:26,985 --> 00:14:31,765
Twenty percent. Half of forty,
since it's used for under 180 days.

237
00:14:32,966 --> 00:14:36,391
Meera sells her old van for more
than the whole block is worth.

238
00:14:36,706 --> 00:14:39,981
The block now holds less
than nothing. What happens?

239
00:14:40,747 --> 00:14:43,707
Imagine a van block holding ₹3 lakh.

240
00:14:43,962 --> 00:14:45,902
Let's follow four different sales.

241
00:14:46,402 --> 00:14:52,987
First, she buys a new van for ₹5 lakh,
and sells the old one for ₹3,60,000.

242
00:14:53,177 --> 00:15:00,177
Three lakh plus five lakh, minus three
lakh sixty thousand, leaves ₹4,40,000.

243
00:15:00,777 --> 00:15:02,537
Depreciation runs on that.

244
00:15:03,177 --> 00:15:08,757
Second, no new van. She sells
her only van for ₹3,60,000.

245
00:15:09,022 --> 00:15:11,967
The block is empty. It
has ceased to exist.

246
00:15:12,510 --> 00:15:15,055
Then there's no
depreciation for that block.

247
00:15:15,475 --> 00:15:18,615
The ₹60,000 over its written down value is

248
00:15:18,665 --> 00:15:21,935
a short-term capital
gain, under section 50.

249
00:15:22,527 --> 00:15:26,412
Why short-term? Because she already
got depreciation on that money.

250
00:15:26,907 --> 00:15:29,272
Gains on depreciable assets in a block are

251
00:15:29,322 --> 00:15:32,282
always short-term,
however long she owned them.

252
00:15:32,793 --> 00:15:36,893
Third. She sells the only
van for just ₹2,40,000.

253
00:15:37,103 --> 00:15:40,693
The block ceases with
₹60,000 still inside.

254
00:15:40,743 --> 00:15:43,248
That's a short-term capital loss.

255
00:15:44,046 --> 00:15:49,891
Fourth. She buys a new van for ₹5 lakh,
and sells a vintage one for ₹9 lakh.

256
00:15:49,941 --> 00:15:52,656
Three plus five is eight,
and nine comes out.

257
00:15:52,706 --> 00:15:54,591
The level stops at zero.

258
00:15:54,641 --> 00:16:00,406
The block lives on at zero, and the extra
₹1 lakh is a short-term capital gain.

259
00:16:01,284 --> 00:16:05,269
Don't charge depreciation on a
block that has ceased, or hit zero.

260
00:16:05,774 --> 00:16:08,029
And the money that comes
out includes scrap value

261
00:16:08,079 --> 00:16:11,244
and insurance money,
not just the sale price.

262
00:16:11,997 --> 00:16:17,342
A machine block holds ₹50,000, and
its only machine is lost in a fire.

263
00:16:17,737 --> 00:16:21,362
Insurance pays ₹30,000. Result?

264
00:16:24,163 --> 00:16:30,313
The block ceases. ₹20,000 is a short-term
capital loss, with no depreciation.

265
00:16:31,659 --> 00:16:35,699
The oven's price tag said ₹3,70,000.

266
00:16:35,954 --> 00:16:38,834
So why did it enter the block at ₹4 lakh?

267
00:16:39,575 --> 00:16:42,645
Because actual cost, under section 43(1),

268
00:16:42,695 --> 00:16:45,535
is everything you spend to
bring the asset into use.

269
00:16:46,010 --> 00:16:51,445
The price, plus ₹10,000 of freight,
plus ₹20,000 for installation.

270
00:16:52,027 --> 00:16:55,392
And it's reduced by any part of
the cost paid by someone else.

271
00:16:55,442 --> 00:17:02,402
If the state paid ₹50,000 of it as a
subsidy, the cost would be ₹3,50,000.

272
00:17:02,915 --> 00:17:04,335
Cash breaks the cost.

273
00:17:04,595 --> 00:17:09,515
Any payment of more than ₹10,000 in a
day in cash is left out of the cost.

274
00:17:09,900 --> 00:17:11,840
No depreciation on it, ever.

275
00:17:12,592 --> 00:17:14,672
Interest has a dividing line too.

276
00:17:14,962 --> 00:17:19,552
Say she borrowed for the oven on 1
April, and started using it on 1 June.

277
00:17:20,012 --> 00:17:22,752
Interest till 1 June is added to the cost.

278
00:17:23,192 --> 00:17:27,422
Interest after that is a normal
expense, under section 36.

279
00:17:28,094 --> 00:17:30,474
Old assets keep their original cost.

280
00:17:30,944 --> 00:17:35,794
If Meera's personal car,
bought for ₹5,25,000,

281
00:17:35,879 --> 00:17:40,814
joins the business, its cost is ₹5,25,000.

282
00:17:41,159 --> 00:17:42,954
Not today's market value.

283
00:17:43,601 --> 00:17:47,701
Two odd cases. A building moved
from personal use is valued at

284
00:17:47,751 --> 00:17:50,701
cost, minus the depreciation
it would have had.

285
00:17:50,751 --> 00:17:53,401
And a gift or an inheritance
takes the previous

286
00:17:53,451 --> 00:17:56,606
owner's cost, minus depreciation since.

287
00:17:57,357 --> 00:18:00,902
One more. If the oven were imported
on credit, and the rupee fell

288
00:18:00,952 --> 00:18:04,802
before she paid, the extra rupees
would be added to its cost.

289
00:18:05,287 --> 00:18:07,142
That's section 43A.

290
00:18:07,632 --> 00:18:10,777
A machine costs ₹6 lakh in all, including

291
00:18:10,827 --> 00:18:14,617
₹50,000 of installation
paid in cash in one day.

292
00:18:14,932 --> 00:18:18,707
The government pays ₹1 lakh. Actual cost?

293
00:18:21,610 --> 00:18:27,610
₹4,50,000. Six lakh, minus the
subsidy, minus the cash payment.

294
00:18:28,735 --> 00:18:32,890
If Meera buys new machines to bake more,
should the law reward her for it?

295
00:18:33,573 --> 00:18:38,113
Under the old rules, yes. If you
manufacture goods and buy new plant or

296
00:18:38,163 --> 00:18:41,453
machinery, you get extra
depreciation in year one.

297
00:18:41,758 --> 00:18:45,383
But there's a catch,
called section 115BAC.

298
00:18:45,887 --> 00:18:49,952
Section 115BAC is the default tax regime.

299
00:18:50,642 --> 00:18:52,612
Lower slab rates, but fewer deductions.

300
00:18:53,052 --> 00:18:56,412
An individual is in it automatically,
unless she opts out.

301
00:18:57,013 --> 00:18:59,838
If she opts out, she gets
additional depreciation.

302
00:19:00,513 --> 00:19:03,138
It's 20% of the cost of
new plant or machinery,

303
00:19:03,188 --> 00:19:06,698
for anyone who makes goods,
or generates power.

304
00:19:07,300 --> 00:19:11,240
So the ₹4 lakh oven gets ₹80,000 extra.

305
00:19:11,765 --> 00:19:17,380
The mixer, used under 180 days,
gets half: ₹20,000 now.

306
00:19:17,855 --> 00:19:23,080
The other ₹20,000 comes next year,
if she's still outside the default regime.

307
00:19:23,673 --> 00:19:27,338
Not every machine qualifies.
Not second hand machines.

308
00:19:27,628 --> 00:19:29,678
Not office machines, like the laptop.

309
00:19:29,983 --> 00:19:31,998
Not road vehicles, like the van.

310
00:19:32,258 --> 00:19:34,673
And not anything fully
deducted in one year.

311
00:19:35,415 --> 00:19:38,655
And a business owner can't hop
between regimes every year.

312
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:42,335
Once she opts out, she gets
only one chance to come back.

313
00:19:42,828 --> 00:19:46,013
What if her profit is too small
to absorb the depreciation?

314
00:19:46,633 --> 00:19:49,553
The unused part is
unabsorbed depreciation.

315
00:19:50,183 --> 00:19:52,133
It's carried forward with no time limit,

316
00:19:52,183 --> 00:19:55,323
and set off against any
head except salaries.

317
00:19:55,905 --> 00:19:57,650
When it's set off, the order matters.

318
00:19:57,930 --> 00:20:00,150
This year's depreciation comes first.

319
00:20:00,425 --> 00:20:04,400
Then old business losses.
Then unabsorbed depreciation.

320
00:20:05,009 --> 00:20:07,514
A printing press buys
a new printing machine.

321
00:20:07,824 --> 00:20:10,184
Is additional depreciation possible?

322
00:20:13,002 --> 00:20:15,802
Yes, if it opts out of 115BAC.

323
00:20:16,337 --> 00:20:20,522
Printing counts as manufacture,
says a CBDT circular.

324
00:20:21,718 --> 00:20:25,943
Meera wants to invent a sugar-free
cake, so she sets up a small lab.

325
00:20:26,313 --> 00:20:28,823
Should research be treated
like any other cost?

326
00:20:29,577 --> 00:20:31,237
The law wants you to do research.

327
00:20:31,607 --> 00:20:36,487
So under section 35, research gets
special treatment, even when it's capital.

328
00:20:36,984 --> 00:20:40,969
Revenue spending on research for her
own business is deducted in full.

329
00:20:41,439 --> 00:20:44,779
That's things like a scientist's
salary, and the ingredients.

330
00:20:45,416 --> 00:20:47,561
And capital spending on
that research is also

331
00:20:47,611 --> 00:20:51,461
deducted in full, in the year
she spends it. Except land.

332
00:20:51,746 --> 00:20:53,146
Land never counts.

333
00:20:53,747 --> 00:20:56,992
She spends ₹3 lakh on
salaries and materials,

334
00:20:57,322 --> 00:21:03,052
and ₹7,50,000 on a lab building,
of which ₹5 lakh is land.

335
00:21:03,597 --> 00:21:08,572
Her deduction is ₹3 lakh plus ₹2,50,000.

336
00:21:08,887 --> 00:21:11,557
That's ₹5,50,000.

337
00:21:12,081 --> 00:21:14,846
Research before the
business starts counts too.

338
00:21:15,246 --> 00:21:17,426
What she spends in the
three years before starting

339
00:21:17,476 --> 00:21:20,296
is deducted in the year
the business begins.

340
00:21:20,800 --> 00:21:26,685
No double counting. A lab asset deducted
under section 35 gets no depreciation.

341
00:21:27,310 --> 00:21:29,645
And if she sells it, the sale price, up to

342
00:21:29,695 --> 00:21:32,570
the deduction, is taxed
as business income.

343
00:21:33,320 --> 00:21:35,105
Paying outsiders is different.

344
00:21:35,465 --> 00:21:40,305
She may give money to an approved
university, research body, company or lab.

345
00:21:40,725 --> 00:21:45,510
That's deductible at 100%, but only
if she opts out of the default regime.

346
00:21:46,005 --> 00:21:50,885
Say Meera also gives ₹1 lakh to an
approved university for food research.

347
00:21:51,035 --> 00:21:55,825
In the default regime,
she still gets ₹5,50,000.

348
00:21:56,015 --> 00:21:59,855
If she opts out, ₹6,50,000.

349
00:22:00,459 --> 00:22:03,644
What if her profit is too small
to use the research deduction?

350
00:22:04,019 --> 00:22:05,454
Can she carry it forward?

351
00:22:08,255 --> 00:22:09,980
Yes, with no time limit.

352
00:22:10,325 --> 00:22:15,170
It's set off like unabsorbed depreciation,
against any head except salaries.

353
00:22:16,398 --> 00:22:20,683
Meera's cousin Anil builds a cold
storage for fruit and vegetables.

354
00:22:21,263 --> 00:22:23,868
What if he could deduct the
whole building in year one?

355
00:22:24,549 --> 00:22:28,409
If you run one of a few businesses
the country badly needs, you can.

356
00:22:29,084 --> 00:22:33,109
Section 35AD gives 100%
of the capital spending

357
00:22:33,159 --> 00:22:35,859
as a deduction, instead of depreciation.

358
00:22:36,634 --> 00:22:38,359
These are specified businesses.

359
00:22:38,909 --> 00:22:43,639
Cold chains, warehouses for farm produce
or sugar, hospitals with a hundred

360
00:22:43,689 --> 00:22:48,804
beds, hotels of two stars and above,
and affordable housing, among others.

361
00:22:49,412 --> 00:22:55,832
Anil spends ₹1.2 crore. ₹40 lakh
is land, which doesn't count.

362
00:22:56,287 --> 00:23:00,932
The other ₹80 lakh is deducted,
including what he spent before opening.

363
00:23:01,427 --> 00:23:06,782
His profit was ₹30 lakh,
so he now has a loss of ₹50 lakh.

364
00:23:07,345 --> 00:23:11,390
That loss can be set off only against
profit from a specified business.

365
00:23:11,620 --> 00:23:14,360
But it can be carried
forward with no time limit.

366
00:23:14,868 --> 00:23:18,528
The conditions bite. He must have
opted out of the default regime.

367
00:23:19,013 --> 00:23:22,848
Any cash payment over
₹10,000 in a day is excluded.

368
00:23:23,273 --> 00:23:26,478
And the asset must be used for
that business for eight years.

369
00:23:27,231 --> 00:23:31,076
What if Anil moves the building to
an ordinary business in year three?

370
00:23:31,536 --> 00:23:33,896
Then the deduction he got, minus normal

371
00:23:33,946 --> 00:23:37,001
depreciation, becomes
his income that year.

372
00:23:37,703 --> 00:23:43,333
Can Anil claim both the 35AD deduction
and depreciation on the same building?

373
00:23:46,201 --> 00:23:50,956
No. Its actual cost becomes nil,
so there's nothing left to depreciate.

374
00:23:52,163 --> 00:23:54,843
Before opening a second
outlet, Meera pays for

375
00:23:54,893 --> 00:23:57,703
a project report,
a market survey and a lawyer.

376
00:23:58,448 --> 00:23:59,748
Capital or revenue?

377
00:24:00,449 --> 00:24:01,449
Neither, quite.

378
00:24:01,729 --> 00:24:05,414
The spending builds the new unit,
but it isn't an asset you can touch.

379
00:24:05,809 --> 00:24:09,214
So section 35D spreads it over five years.

380
00:24:09,718 --> 00:24:13,018
Preliminary expenses are
deducted at one fifth a year,

381
00:24:13,198 --> 00:24:16,543
for five years, starting the
year the new unit begins.

382
00:24:17,150 --> 00:24:21,910
The list is fixed. Feasibility and
project reports, market surveys,

383
00:24:22,030 --> 00:24:25,570
engineering services, and legal
charges for business agreements.

384
00:24:26,035 --> 00:24:28,310
For a company, also its memorandum,

385
00:24:28,585 --> 00:24:31,960
registration fees, and the
costs of a public issue.

386
00:24:32,465 --> 00:24:38,085
Meera spends ₹4 lakh. But there's a
cap: 5% of the cost of the project.

387
00:24:38,135 --> 00:24:43,590
Her project costs ₹60 lakh,
so only ₹3 lakh counts.

388
00:24:43,640 --> 00:24:47,685
She deducts ₹60,000 a
year, for five years.

389
00:24:48,149 --> 00:24:50,589
An Indian company can use 5% of its

390
00:24:50,639 --> 00:24:53,489
capital employed instead,
if that's higher.

391
00:24:53,884 --> 00:24:57,954
And a non-corporate taxpayer must
get her accounts audited to claim it.

392
00:24:58,460 --> 00:25:00,660
Voluntary retirement payments follow the

393
00:25:00,710 --> 00:25:04,700
same five-year pattern,
under section 35DDA.

394
00:25:05,070 --> 00:25:10,645
Pay ₹10 lakh to staff who retire
early, and deduct ₹2 lakh a year.

395
00:25:11,345 --> 00:25:14,730
Meera's books write off the
whole ₹4 lakh this year.

396
00:25:14,995 --> 00:25:16,140
What's the adjustment?

397
00:25:18,901 --> 00:25:22,816
Add back ₹4 lakh, and deduct ₹60,000.

398
00:25:24,084 --> 00:25:25,909
Meera now has twelve employees.

399
00:25:26,329 --> 00:25:28,834
Which of their costs can
she deduct, and when?

400
00:25:29,527 --> 00:25:32,332
Section 36 is a list
of specific deductions.

401
00:25:32,727 --> 00:25:34,372
Most are about people and money.

402
00:25:35,402 --> 00:25:38,737
Insurance, bonus, interest,
staff funds and bad debts.

403
00:25:39,238 --> 00:25:41,198
Insurance on her stock is allowed.

404
00:25:41,488 --> 00:25:45,758
So is health insurance for her staff,
if the premium isn't paid in cash.

405
00:25:46,528 --> 00:25:48,518
Bonus and commission to
employees are allowed

406
00:25:48,568 --> 00:25:51,548
too, unless they replace
a share of profit.

407
00:25:52,052 --> 00:25:54,992
Interest on money borrowed
for the business is allowed.

408
00:25:55,382 --> 00:26:00,637
But for a new asset, interest before it's
first used goes into its cost, as we saw.

409
00:26:01,139 --> 00:26:03,584
Her contribution to her
staff's pension scheme,

410
00:26:03,634 --> 00:26:07,664
the NPS, is allowed up to 14% of salary.

411
00:26:07,954 --> 00:26:10,924
Salary means basic plus
dearness allowance.

412
00:26:10,994 --> 00:26:17,994
With basic of ₹10 lakh and DA of
₹4 lakh, the limit is ₹1,96,000.

413
00:26:19,859 --> 00:26:24,574
She actually paid ₹2 lakh.
So ₹4,000 is disallowed.

414
00:26:25,013 --> 00:26:26,868
Here's the one examiners love.

415
00:26:27,738 --> 00:26:30,588
Employees' own PF share,
cut from their salary,

416
00:26:30,718 --> 00:26:33,848
must reach the fund by the
due date under the PF law.

417
00:26:34,263 --> 00:26:36,048
That's the fifteenth of the next month.

418
00:26:36,273 --> 00:26:38,198
Late means never deductible.

419
00:26:38,965 --> 00:26:41,065
Bad debts are allowed once
they're written off in

420
00:26:41,115 --> 00:26:44,365
the books, if the debt was
counted as income earlier.

421
00:26:44,910 --> 00:26:50,115
If she later recovers one, the recovery
becomes income again, under section 41(4).

422
00:26:50,849 --> 00:26:53,494
And a share trader can deduct
the securities transaction

423
00:26:53,544 --> 00:26:57,429
tax he pays, when his trading
profit is business income.

424
00:26:57,928 --> 00:27:03,913
Meera cuts ₹15,000 of PF from May
salaries, and deposits it on 30 June.

425
00:27:04,338 --> 00:27:05,338
Allowed?

426
00:27:07,871 --> 00:27:10,176
No. It was due by 15 June.

427
00:27:10,791 --> 00:27:15,646
Section 43B's extra time doesn't
apply to employees' contributions.

428
00:27:16,995 --> 00:27:19,480
Meera pays a food blogger
to taste her cakes.

429
00:27:19,855 --> 00:27:23,310
No section mentions
bloggers. Is that cost lost?

430
00:27:24,016 --> 00:27:26,861
No. Section 37 is the last door.

431
00:27:27,261 --> 00:27:30,141
Any expense not covered
by sections 30 to 36

432
00:27:30,191 --> 00:27:33,251
can still pass, if it
meets a few conditions.

433
00:27:33,829 --> 00:27:36,989
It must be laid out wholly and
exclusively for the business.

434
00:27:37,369 --> 00:27:40,494
It must not be capital. And
it must not be personal.

435
00:27:41,093 --> 00:27:44,978
Notice the words. Wholly and
exclusively, not necessarily.

436
00:27:45,473 --> 00:27:47,818
Even if you spend unwisely, the expense is

437
00:27:47,868 --> 00:27:50,403
allowed, as long as it
was for the business.

438
00:27:50,902 --> 00:27:53,907
Illegal spending never passes. A bribe.

439
00:27:54,177 --> 00:27:55,987
A penalty for breaking a law.

440
00:27:56,182 --> 00:28:00,057
Or freebies that doctors aren't
allowed to accept from drug companies.

441
00:28:00,832 --> 00:28:04,657
Since April 2025, money
paid to settle proceedings

442
00:28:04,707 --> 00:28:08,157
under the SEBI and Competition
laws is blocked too.

443
00:28:08,552 --> 00:28:11,897
But a penalty for late delivery
under a contract is fine.

444
00:28:12,367 --> 00:28:14,667
It's compensation, not a fine.

445
00:28:15,176 --> 00:28:20,976
Two more rules. CSR spending by a company
is never allowed under section 37.

446
00:28:21,471 --> 00:28:25,666
And nobody can deduct an advertisement
in a political party's souvenir.

447
00:28:26,167 --> 00:28:31,987
Some costs that do pass. Keyman insurance
premium on a key person, even a partner.

448
00:28:32,367 --> 00:28:36,217
The food blogger's fee. Legal
fees to defend her business.

449
00:28:36,855 --> 00:28:42,200
Meera's van gets a ₹2,000 fine for
parking on the road. Deductible?

450
00:28:44,929 --> 00:28:47,344
No. It's a penalty for breaking the law.

451
00:28:48,547 --> 00:28:54,532
Meera pays a chef-consultant ₹1,50,000,
and forgets to deduct TDS.

452
00:28:55,027 --> 00:28:58,522
The cost is real. Why would
the law cut her deduction?

453
00:28:59,235 --> 00:29:03,255
Because tax deducted at source is
how the government follows the money.

454
00:29:03,680 --> 00:29:08,330
If she doesn't deduct it, the law holds
back part of her deduction until she does.

455
00:29:08,831 --> 00:29:11,276
For a payment to a resident, 30% of the

456
00:29:11,326 --> 00:29:15,116
expense is disallowed,
under section 40(a)(ia).

457
00:29:15,636 --> 00:29:20,796
For a payment to a non-resident,
under section 40(a)(i), it's 100%.

458
00:29:21,401 --> 00:29:28,301
So 30% of ₹1,50,000, which
is ₹45,000, is added back.

459
00:29:28,816 --> 00:29:34,676
If she deducts and pays the tax next year,
that ₹45,000 is allowed next year.

460
00:29:35,260 --> 00:29:39,900
The deadline is generous. Tax deducted
this year can be paid any time before

461
00:29:39,950 --> 00:29:43,900
the due date of the return,
under section 139(1).

462
00:29:44,408 --> 00:29:49,088
There's one escape. If the payee has
filed his return, included the income and

463
00:29:49,138 --> 00:29:52,848
paid the tax, she's treated as
having deducted it on that date.

464
00:29:53,218 --> 00:29:55,078
An accountant must certify it.

465
00:29:55,581 --> 00:29:57,861
Students forget section 40(a)(ii).

466
00:29:58,286 --> 00:30:03,121
Income tax itself is never a business
expense, including surcharge and cess.

467
00:30:03,631 --> 00:30:06,411
You can't deduct the tax
on profit from the profit.

468
00:30:07,156 --> 00:30:09,701
And when an employer pays
the tax on an employee's

469
00:30:09,751 --> 00:30:13,261
non-cash perks, that tax
isn't deductible either.

470
00:30:13,748 --> 00:30:17,273
Write it like this. Thirty percent
of any sum payable to a resident

471
00:30:17,323 --> 00:30:21,053
is disallowed, if tax at
source is not deducted.

472
00:30:21,478 --> 00:30:26,278
The same applies if it's not paid by
the due date under section 139(1).

473
00:30:27,028 --> 00:30:31,773
A company pays salary of ₹14
lakh without deducting TDS.

474
00:30:32,268 --> 00:30:33,818
How much is disallowed?

475
00:30:36,629 --> 00:30:40,204
30%, which is ₹4,20,000.

476
00:30:41,431 --> 00:30:45,256
Meera's brother Rohan joins,
and the bakery becomes a firm.

477
00:30:45,626 --> 00:30:47,776
The firm pays both of them a salary.

478
00:30:48,066 --> 00:30:50,736
Can a firm pay its own
owners, and deduct it?

479
00:30:51,521 --> 00:30:54,041
A firm and its partners
are the same people.

480
00:30:54,396 --> 00:30:56,621
If you could pay yourself
any salary, you could

481
00:30:56,671 --> 00:30:59,776
drain every rupee out,
and leave nothing to tax.

482
00:31:00,284 --> 00:31:02,904
So section 40(b) allows it, within limits.

483
00:31:03,774 --> 00:31:05,424
Salary only to working partners.

484
00:31:05,734 --> 00:31:09,439
It must be written in the deed, and
only for the period after the deed.

485
00:31:10,041 --> 00:31:15,041
Interest on a partner's capital is
allowed up to 12% a year, simple interest.

486
00:31:15,461 --> 00:31:17,181
Anything more is disallowed.

487
00:31:17,669 --> 00:31:20,474
Book profit is the firm's
net profit, worked out

488
00:31:20,524 --> 00:31:23,324
under the tax rules,
before the partners' salary.

489
00:31:23,821 --> 00:31:25,781
The salary limit works on book profit.

490
00:31:25,831 --> 00:31:32,831
On the first ₹6 lakh, or on a loss,
it's ₹3 lakh or 90%, whichever is higher.

491
00:31:33,191 --> 00:31:35,751
On the rest, it's 60%.

492
00:31:36,448 --> 00:31:41,813
The firm earns ₹17 lakh,
before ₹1,50,000 of depreciation,

493
00:31:41,863 --> 00:31:45,113
and before the partners'
salary and interest.

494
00:31:46,063 --> 00:31:53,063
Interest at 15% on ₹5 lakh is ₹75,000,
but only 12%, ₹60,000, is allowed.

495
00:31:55,678 --> 00:31:59,518
So the book profit is ₹14,90,000.

496
00:32:00,172 --> 00:32:06,052
The limit is 90% of ₹6
lakh, which is ₹5,40,000.

497
00:32:06,432 --> 00:32:13,432
Plus 60% of the other ₹8,90,000,
which is ₹5,34,000.

498
00:32:13,827 --> 00:32:17,102
Total, ₹10,74,000.

499
00:32:17,667 --> 00:32:20,727
The partners were paid
₹40,000 a month each.

500
00:32:21,092 --> 00:32:24,487
That's ₹9,60,000, inside the limit.

501
00:32:24,822 --> 00:32:26,052
All of it is allowed.

502
00:32:26,651 --> 00:32:31,631
On the other side, Rohan pays tax on the
salary and interest the firm could deduct.

503
00:32:31,996 --> 00:32:34,011
That's business income in his hands.

504
00:32:34,356 --> 00:32:36,696
His share of the firm's profit is exempt.

505
00:32:37,196 --> 00:32:38,681
What if the firm makes a loss?

506
00:32:39,091 --> 00:32:43,746
It can still pay its working partners
up to ₹3 lakh in all, and deduct it.

507
00:32:44,426 --> 00:32:48,651
The deed allows salary from 1 April,
but it was signed on 1 July.

508
00:32:49,081 --> 00:32:50,771
From when is salary allowed?

509
00:32:53,569 --> 00:32:58,189
From 1 July. A deed can't authorise
salary for the period before it.

510
00:32:59,388 --> 00:33:03,793
Meera buys flour from her
husband's mill at ₹48 a kilo.

511
00:33:04,058 --> 00:33:07,938
The market price is ₹40.
What will the officer say?

512
00:33:08,677 --> 00:33:11,117
If you overpay a relative,
you quietly move

513
00:33:11,167 --> 00:33:13,497
profit from one family member to another.

514
00:33:13,897 --> 00:33:17,677
So section 40A(2) lets the
officer disallow the excess.

515
00:33:18,217 --> 00:33:20,212
Here, ₹8 a kilo.

516
00:33:20,713 --> 00:33:25,813
These are specified persons. For an
individual, relatives: spouse, brother,

517
00:33:25,883 --> 00:33:29,193
sister, and anyone in the
direct line, up or down.

518
00:33:29,613 --> 00:33:33,723
Also businesses where she or
a relative holds at least 20%.

519
00:33:34,324 --> 00:33:40,109
Now cash. Under section 40A(3),
if she pays one person more than ₹10,000

520
00:33:40,159 --> 00:33:43,589
in cash in a single day, the
whole payment is disallowed.

521
00:33:44,014 --> 00:33:45,374
Not just the excess.

522
00:33:45,863 --> 00:33:49,513
Four cash payments of
₹8,000 to the same supplier,

523
00:33:49,628 --> 00:33:52,853
at ten, at noon, at three and at six.

524
00:33:53,548 --> 00:33:58,273
Each is under the limit,
but the day's total is ₹32,000.

525
00:33:58,583 --> 00:34:01,373
All ₹32,000 is disallowed.

526
00:34:01,966 --> 00:34:04,611
Safe modes are an account
payee cheque or draft,

527
00:34:04,766 --> 00:34:11,691
and electronic payment: card,
net banking, UPI, IMPS, NEFT, RTGS.

528
00:34:12,231 --> 00:34:17,676
For a transporter hiring out goods
carriages, the cash limit is ₹35,000.

529
00:34:18,176 --> 00:34:22,221
What if an expense was allowed last year,
and she pays it in cash this year?

530
00:34:22,621 --> 00:34:27,551
Then that cash payment becomes income
this year, under section 40A(3A).

531
00:34:28,289 --> 00:34:32,834
Rule 6DD gives exceptions.
Paying a bank or the government.

532
00:34:33,209 --> 00:34:36,074
Buying wheat directly from
the farmer who grew it.

533
00:34:36,164 --> 00:34:37,994
Paying in a village with no bank.

534
00:34:38,214 --> 00:34:41,404
And retirement benefits up to ₹50,000.

535
00:34:41,913 --> 00:34:47,753
Two last ones. A provision for gratuity
isn't deductible, under section 40A(7).

536
00:34:48,178 --> 00:34:51,533
Only payments are,
or contributions to an approved fund.

537
00:34:51,908 --> 00:34:56,743
And contributions to unapproved staff
funds are blocked, under 40A(9).

538
00:34:57,246 --> 00:34:58,331
Write it like this.

539
00:34:58,741 --> 00:35:03,491
Expenditure paid to a person in a day,
otherwise than by account payee cheque,

540
00:35:03,566 --> 00:35:09,196
draft or prescribed electronic mode,
exceeding ₹10,000, is disallowed.

541
00:35:09,931 --> 00:35:16,221
Meera buys ₹60,000 of milk in cash from
a dairy farmer, in one day. Disallowed?

542
00:35:18,924 --> 00:35:24,439
No. Buying dairy produce from the
producer is an exception under Rule 6DD.

543
00:35:25,711 --> 00:35:30,011
On 31 March, Meera owes
₹80,000 of staff bonus.

544
00:35:30,386 --> 00:35:34,216
It's in her books. Can she deduct
it this year, before paying it?

545
00:35:34,917 --> 00:35:37,312
Deduct now, or only after paying?

546
00:35:39,654 --> 00:35:43,474
If you could claim dues without paying,
you could delay paying for years.

547
00:35:43,974 --> 00:35:49,339
So section 43B says certain sums are
deductible only when actually paid.

548
00:35:49,834 --> 00:35:52,734
The list: taxes, duties, cess and fees.

549
00:35:52,784 --> 00:35:56,674
The employer's contributions
to PF and other staff funds.

550
00:35:56,919 --> 00:36:01,554
Bonus and commission. Interest to banks
and public financial institutions.

551
00:36:01,979 --> 00:36:04,789
Leave encashment. And Indian Railways.

552
00:36:05,389 --> 00:36:09,534
But there's a grace period. If she
pays before the due date of her return,

553
00:36:09,664 --> 00:36:13,329
under section 139(1),
it's still allowed this year.

554
00:36:13,821 --> 00:36:20,146
Meera's return is due on 31 October
2026, because she's under tax audit.

555
00:36:20,276 --> 00:36:24,386
If the bonus is paid on 15
October, it's allowed this year.

556
00:36:24,436 --> 00:36:27,956
Paid on 15 November,
it moves to next year.

557
00:36:28,516 --> 00:36:32,656
Converting unpaid bank interest
into a new loan is not payment.

558
00:36:33,106 --> 00:36:36,756
It's allowed only as she actually
repays that converted loan.

559
00:36:37,491 --> 00:36:40,276
Then the newest clause, 43B(h).

560
00:36:40,326 --> 00:36:43,101
Money owed to a micro or small enterprise

561
00:36:43,151 --> 00:36:45,876
must be paid within the
time in the MSME law.

562
00:36:46,186 --> 00:36:49,806
Otherwise, it's deductible
only in the year it's paid.

563
00:36:50,390 --> 00:36:53,615
She buys boxes worth ₹10,000 from a micro

564
00:36:53,665 --> 00:36:58,435
enterprise on 1 March 2026,
payable by 5 April.

565
00:36:58,845 --> 00:37:01,455
Paid on 2 April, it's allowed this year.

566
00:37:01,845 --> 00:37:04,830
Paid on 20 April, it moves to next year.

567
00:37:05,429 --> 00:37:10,814
Notice the difference. Ordinary 43B
items get grace till the return date.

568
00:37:11,104 --> 00:37:13,174
MSME dues get no such grace.

569
00:37:13,449 --> 00:37:16,454
And the rule doesn't apply
to medium enterprises at all.

570
00:37:16,945 --> 00:37:20,730
Meera collects GST in March,
and pays it on 20 April.

571
00:37:21,075 --> 00:37:22,185
Allowed this year?

572
00:37:24,925 --> 00:37:27,730
Yes. It's paid before the
due date of the return.

573
00:37:28,925 --> 00:37:33,630
Years ago, Meera deducted a
₹35,000 bill from her supplier.

574
00:37:34,140 --> 00:37:38,400
Now the supplier says, forget it,
you needn't pay. Is that a gift?

575
00:37:39,126 --> 00:37:41,206
She already got a deduction for that bill.

576
00:37:41,476 --> 00:37:44,146
If she never pays it, the
deduction was for nothing.

577
00:37:44,476 --> 00:37:46,001
So the law takes it back.

578
00:37:46,504 --> 00:37:50,664
That's a remission of a trading
liability, under section 41(1).

579
00:37:51,084 --> 00:37:55,069
The amount waived becomes business
income, in the year it's waived.

580
00:37:55,667 --> 00:37:58,832
It applies even if she writes
the liability back herself.

581
00:37:59,107 --> 00:38:01,432
And even if the business
has already closed.

582
00:38:01,787 --> 00:38:04,742
A successor in the business
is taxed the same way.

583
00:38:05,245 --> 00:38:07,930
The same idea runs through section 41.

584
00:38:08,425 --> 00:38:11,510
A bad debt recovered later, under 41(4).

585
00:38:11,910 --> 00:38:14,770
A research asset sold, under 41(3).

586
00:38:15,050 --> 00:38:18,085
Whatever was deducted
and comes back, is taxed.

587
00:38:18,582 --> 00:38:22,347
Now a different business. A
builder holds flats as his stock.

588
00:38:22,607 --> 00:38:25,647
He sells one to a friend for ₹1 crore.

589
00:38:25,962 --> 00:38:29,542
But the stamp duty value is ₹1.2 crore.

590
00:38:30,139 --> 00:38:35,764
Under section 43CA, the stamp duty
value is taken as the sale price.

591
00:38:36,199 --> 00:38:39,619
Unless it's within 110%
of the actual price.

592
00:38:40,222 --> 00:38:45,927
₹1.2 crore is more than 110% of ₹1 crore.

593
00:38:46,177 --> 00:38:49,687
So the sale is taken at ₹1.2 crore.

594
00:38:50,127 --> 00:38:57,127
If the stamp value were ₹1.08 crore,
his own price of ₹1 crore would stand.

595
00:38:57,736 --> 00:39:00,056
Sometimes the price is agreed earlier,

596
00:39:00,221 --> 00:39:03,296
and part is paid by a
non-cash mode by that date.

597
00:39:03,671 --> 00:39:06,706
Then the stamp value on
the agreement date is used.

598
00:39:07,207 --> 00:39:13,597
Meera writes off a supplier's balance of
₹20,000 as no longer payable. Taxable?

599
00:39:16,350 --> 00:39:21,860
Yes. Writing back a trading liability
is a remission, under section 41(1).

600
00:39:23,065 --> 00:39:27,060
Does every shopkeeper in India have
to keep books, and get them audited?

601
00:39:27,795 --> 00:39:32,440
No. You need books only above
certain limits, under section 44AA.

602
00:39:32,970 --> 00:39:37,830
And you need an audit only above
higher limits, under section 44AB.

603
00:39:38,313 --> 00:39:43,643
For specified professions, like doctors,
lawyers and accountants, prescribed books

604
00:39:43,693 --> 00:39:49,643
are needed if gross receipts crossed
₹1,50,000 in each of the last three years.

605
00:39:50,043 --> 00:39:53,668
For other businesses run by an
individual, there are two limits.

606
00:39:54,323 --> 00:39:57,688
Books are needed if
income crossed ₹2,50,000,

607
00:39:57,738 --> 00:40:02,868
or turnover crossed ₹25 lakh,
in any of the last three years.

608
00:40:03,423 --> 00:40:07,523
Tax audit is needed when business
turnover is over ₹1 crore.

609
00:40:07,843 --> 00:40:13,853
That rises to ₹10 crore if cash receipts
and cash payments are each within 5%.

610
00:40:13,903 --> 00:40:17,433
For a profession, the limit is ₹50 lakh.

611
00:40:18,009 --> 00:40:24,094
Meera's turnover is ₹2.4 crore,
and 12% comes in cash at the counter.

612
00:40:24,694 --> 00:40:29,034
That's over 5%, so the ₹10
crore limit isn't open to her.

613
00:40:29,349 --> 00:40:30,879
She needs a tax audit.

614
00:40:31,563 --> 00:40:37,388
The audit report is due by 30 September
2026, a month before her return.

615
00:40:37,803 --> 00:40:43,488
Miss it, and the penalty is half a
percent of turnover, up to ₹1,50,000.

616
00:40:43,818 --> 00:40:47,133
Not keeping books costs ₹25,000.

617
00:40:47,663 --> 00:40:50,463
A trader with ₹8 crore
of turnover receives

618
00:40:50,513 --> 00:40:53,773
and pays everything
through the bank. Audit?

619
00:40:56,574 --> 00:41:02,189
No. Cash is within 5% on both
sides, so his limit is ₹10 crore.

620
00:41:03,393 --> 00:41:05,913
A small grocery shop has no accountant.

621
00:41:06,283 --> 00:41:08,763
How can the tax department
know its profit?

622
00:41:09,517 --> 00:41:13,617
It can't, easily. So for small
businesses, the law offers a deal.

623
00:41:14,057 --> 00:41:17,702
Declare a fixed share of your turnover
as profit, and skip the books.

624
00:41:18,196 --> 00:41:21,981
That's presumptive taxation,
under section 44AD.

625
00:41:22,191 --> 00:41:25,041
Profit is taken as 8% of turnover.

626
00:41:25,091 --> 00:41:29,776
Or 6%, on the part received
through banks or digital modes.

627
00:41:30,374 --> 00:41:35,664
Who can use it? A resident individual,
HUF, or partnership firm, but not an LLP.

628
00:41:36,309 --> 00:41:42,834
Turnover up to ₹2 crore. Or up to ₹3
crore, if cash receipts are within 5%.

629
00:41:43,327 --> 00:41:44,592
It's not for everyone.

630
00:41:44,902 --> 00:41:49,332
Not for professionals, commission or
brokerage earners, or agency businesses.

631
00:41:49,582 --> 00:41:52,742
And not for goods carriages,
which have their own scheme.

632
00:41:53,498 --> 00:41:58,078
Sunil's grocery turns over ₹2,98,50,000.

633
00:41:58,458 --> 00:42:02,698
Only ₹14 lakh comes in
cash, which is under 5%.

634
00:42:03,038 --> 00:42:09,058
So he's eligible. 8% of
₹14 lakh is ₹1,12,000.

635
00:42:09,593 --> 00:42:16,593
6% of the rest is ₹17,07,000.
His income is ₹18,19,000.

636
00:42:17,754 --> 00:42:21,459
That's it. Every deduction
from sections 30 to 38 counts

637
00:42:21,509 --> 00:42:24,419
as already given, depreciation included.

638
00:42:24,909 --> 00:42:28,359
Even a firm can't deduct
partners' salary and interest.

639
00:42:28,804 --> 00:42:32,409
And advance tax is due
in one go, by 15 March.

640
00:42:32,909 --> 00:42:36,674
What if he uses the scheme one year,
then declares less within the next five?

641
00:42:37,154 --> 00:42:39,474
He's locked out for five years after that.

642
00:42:39,729 --> 00:42:42,034
He must keep books,
and get an audit, if his

643
00:42:42,084 --> 00:42:44,799
income is above the basic exemption limit.

644
00:42:45,543 --> 00:42:49,793
Write it like this. The profit is
deemed to be 8% of total turnover.

645
00:42:49,983 --> 00:42:53,013
For turnover received
by account payee cheque,

646
00:42:53,068 --> 00:42:56,963
draft, or prescribed
electronic modes, it's 6%.

647
00:42:57,745 --> 00:43:02,885
Meera's turnover is ₹2.4
crore, and 12% is cash.

648
00:43:03,280 --> 00:43:05,535
Can she use section 44AD?

649
00:43:08,303 --> 00:43:12,998
No. Above ₹2 crore, cash
receipts must be within 5%.

650
00:43:14,206 --> 00:43:19,006
Kabir the dentist earns ₹60 lakh a
year, and Ravi owns three trucks.

651
00:43:19,336 --> 00:43:20,841
Do they get a shortcut too?

652
00:43:21,538 --> 00:43:24,883
Yes. If you're a professional,
there's section 44ADA.

653
00:43:25,538 --> 00:43:29,053
If you run goods carriages,
there's section 44AE.

654
00:43:29,560 --> 00:43:34,220
Under 44ADA, half the gross
receipts is taken as profit.

655
00:43:34,710 --> 00:43:40,350
It's for a resident individual or firm,
but not an LLP, in a specified profession.

656
00:43:40,979 --> 00:43:45,159
Kabir's receipts are ₹60
lakh, with 3% in cash.

657
00:43:45,624 --> 00:43:49,559
That's within ₹75 lakh,
with cash under 5%.

658
00:43:49,609 --> 00:43:52,559
So his income is ₹30 lakh.

659
00:43:53,014 --> 00:43:59,149
If 8% came in cash, his limit would be ₹50
lakh, and he'd need books and an audit.

660
00:43:59,729 --> 00:44:04,449
Under 44AE, a person owning at
most ten goods carriages, at any

661
00:44:04,499 --> 00:44:08,179
time in the year, declares a fixed
amount per vehicle, per month.

662
00:44:08,772 --> 00:44:10,937
Ravi has two light trucks all year.

663
00:44:11,317 --> 00:44:17,417
That's two times twelve months,
times ₹7,500, or ₹1,80,000.

664
00:44:17,832 --> 00:44:21,037
On 29 August he buys a 15 ton heavy truck.

665
00:44:21,402 --> 00:44:23,297
August to March is eight months.

666
00:44:23,692 --> 00:44:29,097
Eight times fifteen,
times ₹1,000, is ₹1,20,000.

667
00:44:29,292 --> 00:44:31,442
Total, ₹3,00,000.

668
00:44:32,197 --> 00:44:34,742
Months count from ownership, not use.

669
00:44:35,082 --> 00:44:39,622
A truck bought in April but driven
only from July still counts from April.

670
00:44:39,952 --> 00:44:42,392
And any part of a month
counts as a whole month.

671
00:44:43,137 --> 00:44:44,782
One difference worth remembering.

672
00:44:44,832 --> 00:44:48,702
Under 44AE, a firm can
still deduct partners'

673
00:44:48,752 --> 00:44:51,462
salary and interest, within section 40(b).

674
00:44:51,512 --> 00:44:56,137
Under 44AD and 44ADA, it can't.

675
00:44:56,643 --> 00:45:01,323
Kabir declares ₹22 lakh
on receipts of ₹50 lakh,

676
00:45:01,473 --> 00:45:03,823
and his income is above
the exemption limit.

677
00:45:04,228 --> 00:45:05,258
What must he do?

678
00:45:08,091 --> 00:45:13,971
Keep books under section 44AA, and
get them audited under section 44AB.

679
00:45:15,175 --> 00:45:18,200
Back to Meera. Her books say ₹12 lakh.

680
00:45:18,315 --> 00:45:21,180
Let's put every line
through the machine. Ready?

681
00:45:21,768 --> 00:45:24,648
Start with the net profit, ₹12 lakh.

682
00:45:25,048 --> 00:45:27,568
First, add back what
the law doesn't allow.

683
00:45:27,963 --> 00:45:34,908
Book depreciation, ₹3,10,000.
Income tax, ₹1,20,000.

684
00:45:35,093 --> 00:45:39,358
And the new oven, ₹4 lakh,
wrongly written off as an expense.

685
00:45:40,028 --> 00:45:45,473
Then the disallowances. Cash to
the packaging supplier, ₹24,000.

686
00:45:45,858 --> 00:45:51,153
30% of the consultant's
fee without TDS, ₹45,000.

687
00:45:51,823 --> 00:45:55,253
Bonus paid after the return date, ₹80,000.

688
00:45:55,538 --> 00:45:58,953
And the late MSME bill, ₹60,000.

689
00:45:59,554 --> 00:46:04,239
And the rest. Her family trip,
₹40,000, which is personal.

690
00:46:04,734 --> 00:46:07,939
The food safety penalty, ₹15,000.

691
00:46:08,214 --> 00:46:11,639
And the provision for gratuity, ₹50,000.

692
00:46:11,914 --> 00:46:15,964
Our total so far is ₹23,44,000.

693
00:46:16,521 --> 00:46:18,506
Now deduct what the books missed.

694
00:46:18,961 --> 00:46:23,346
Depreciation as per the Act, ₹2,85,000.

695
00:46:23,686 --> 00:46:27,726
And last year's bonus,
paid this year, ₹70,000.

696
00:46:28,036 --> 00:46:31,266
That leaves ₹19,89,000.

697
00:46:31,769 --> 00:46:33,409
Take out other heads' income.

698
00:46:33,894 --> 00:46:39,609
₹30,000 of bank interest, and
₹1,20,000 of rent from a flat.

699
00:46:40,029 --> 00:46:43,344
That leaves ₹18,39,000.

700
00:46:43,862 --> 00:46:49,592
Finally, add deemed income. A
bad debt recovered, ₹25,000.

701
00:46:49,887 --> 00:46:53,872
And the supplier's waived bill, ₹35,000.

702
00:46:54,182 --> 00:46:58,387
Meera's business income is ₹18,99,000.

703
00:46:58,940 --> 00:47:01,185
And if she'd opted out
of the default regime?

704
00:47:01,720 --> 00:47:06,185
Additional depreciation of ₹1
lakh, on the oven and the mixer.

705
00:47:06,710 --> 00:47:10,555
Her income would be ₹17,99,000.

706
00:47:11,255 --> 00:47:13,420
So, the whole chapter in one breath.

707
00:47:13,860 --> 00:47:15,960
Section 28 decides what's caught.

708
00:47:16,245 --> 00:47:18,160
The books are only the starting point.

709
00:47:18,720 --> 00:47:23,440
Capital becomes depreciation,
in blocks, at half rate under 180 days.

710
00:47:24,044 --> 00:47:26,829
Sections 30 to 37 let costs in.

711
00:47:26,899 --> 00:47:31,689
Sections 40, 40A and 43B keep
some out, or make them wait.

712
00:47:31,819 --> 00:47:34,649
Section 41 brings back what returns.

713
00:47:34,739 --> 00:47:37,999
And small businesses can take
the presumptive shortcut.

714
00:47:38,606 --> 00:47:41,126
Every adjustment answers
one of three questions.

715
00:47:41,541 --> 00:47:43,786
Is it business income? Is it allowed?

716
00:47:44,111 --> 00:47:49,636
Is it allowed this year? Ask those three,
and no PGBP question can surprise you.

